course: dictionary
lesson: 10478
title: Same word, different jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one to use. «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse or be full of bad air.» Here a tunnel is a long passage under the ground or through a hill, because the mines and caves are underground too.
explain: Look at the clues in the next sentence. «Some roads have small toad tunnels» tells you that a toad tunnel is a passage under a road, so toads can cross safely. The clue is the word roads: a tunnel is a place you go through, not a thing you do.
explain: Tunnel can also be an action word. To tunnel means to dig a tunnel through or under something, as moles and rabbits do. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing, or is it describing something someone is doing? That question tells you whether it is a noun or a verb.
explain: Some phrases use tunnel in a special way. «Light at the end of the tunnel» does not mean a real light in a real tunnel. It means there is hope that a hard time will end. When a meaning seems strange, check the whole phrase, not just one word.
fact: A noun names a thing, and a verb names an action.
fact: Tunnel is a noun when it means a long passage under the ground, through a hill, or under water.
fact: Tunnel is a verb when it means to dig a tunnel through or under something.
fact: Animals such as moles, rabbits and earthworms make tunnels, and earthworm tunnels help air and water get into soil.
fact: «Light at the end of the tunnel» means there is hope that a hard time will end.
exercise: «Some roads have small toad tunnels.» What are toad tunnels for? | Toads eating insects in a garden | Toads sleeping in the sun | Toads swimming in a deep pond | *Toads crossing safely under a road
exercise: In «She does not dig a tunnel under the dune», what kind of word is tunnel? | A question word | *A noun, a thing | An adjective, a describing word | A verb, an action
exercise: «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse.» Why should you never go into them? | *They can collapse | They are full of toys | They are too bright inside | They are too close to school
test: What does «to tunnel» mean? | To fly high over a hill | To paint a wall in bright colours | To walk very slowly on a path | *To dig a tunnel through or under something
test: What does «light at the end of the tunnel» mean? | A hole in the wall of a house | *Hope that a hard time will end | A torch you must carry into a cave | A very long train coming closer
exam: Which sentence uses tunnel as a verb, an action? | Old mines are full of bad air | *The mole will tunnel under the lawn tonight | The toads crossed the road on a mild night | The train went into the tunnel
